::GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q01::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.5<br><br>Question:<br>Point A(2, 3) is translated by (x, y) → (x + 4, y - 1). What is A'? {
= (6, 2)#Correct. Add 4 to the x-coordinate and subtract 1 from the y-coordinate.
~ (-2, 4)#This subtracts 4 from x and adds 1 to y instead of following the rule.
~ (6, 4)#This adds 4 to x but adds 1 to y instead of subtracting 1.
~ (2, 7)#This changes only the y-coordinate and does not apply the rule to x.
}

::GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q02::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.5<br><br>Question:<br>Point B(-3, 5) is reflected across the y-axis. What is B'? {
~ (-3, -5)#This is a reflection across the x-axis, not the y-axis.
= (3, 5)#Correct. Reflection across the y-axis changes the sign of x and keeps y the same.
~ (5, -3)#This switches the coordinates and changes the wrong value.
~ (3, -5)#This changes both signs, which is not a y-axis reflection.
}

::GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q03::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.5<br><br>Question:<br>Point C(4, -1) is rotated 90 degrees counterclockwise about the origin. What is C'? {
~ (-1, -4)#This is the 90 degree clockwise rotation result.
~ (-4, 1)#This changes both signs but does not use the 90 degree counterclockwise rule.
= (1, 4)#Correct. Using (x, y) → (-y, x), C(4, -1) maps to (1, 4).
~ (-1, 4)#This keeps the x-value sign pattern incorrectly.
}

::GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q04::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.5<br><br>Question:<br>Point D(1, 2) is reflected across the x-axis and then translated 3 units right. What is D''? {
~ (1, -2)#This is only the result after the reflection step.
~ (-2, 2)#This translates in the wrong direction and misses the reflection result.
~ (4, 2)#This translates the original point but does not reflect first.
= (4, -2)#Correct. Reflect to (1, -2), then translate right to (4, -2).
}

::GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q05::
Question ID:<br>GEO_U02_L04_GP_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.GEO.CGM.5<br><br>Question:<br>When drawing the image of triangle ABC after a transformation, what must be transformed? {
~ Only vertex A#Transforming one vertex is not enough to draw the whole image.
= All vertices A, B, and C#Correct. Every vertex must be transformed before drawing the image.
~ Only the longest side#A side alone does not determine all transformed vertices.
~ Only the point closest to the origin#Distance from the origin does not decide which points must be transformed.
}
